@node Lexical conventions
@nodedescription Lexical conventions used in Scheme programs.
@chapter Lexical conventions

This section gives an informal account of some of the lexical
conventions used in writing Scheme programs.  For a formal syntax of
Scheme, see sec@value{shy}tion 7.1.

@node Identifiers
@section Identifiers

An identifier is any sequence of letters, digits, and @quotedbl{extended
identifier char@value{shy}ac@value{shy}ters} provided that it does not
have a prefix which is a valid number.  How@value{shy}ever, the @code{.}
token (a single period) used in the list syntax is not an identifier.

All implementations of Scheme must support the following extended
iden@value{shy}tifier characters:

@example
! $ % & * + - . / : < = > ? @atchar{} ^ _ ~
@end example

Alternatively, an identifier can be represented by a sequence of zero or
more characters enclosed within vertical lines (@code{|}), analogous to
string lit@value{shy}er@value{shy}als.  Any character, including
whitespace characters, but excluding the back@value{shy}slash and
vertical line characters, can appear verbatim in such an
iden@value{shy}tifier.  In addition, characters can be specified using
either an @angle{inline hex escape} or the same escapes available in
strings.

For example, the identifier @hc{|H\x65;llo|} is the same identifier as
@code{Hello}, and in an implementation that supports the appropriate
Unicode character the identifier @hc{|\x3BB;|} is the same as the
identifier @code{@U{03BB}}.  What is more, @hc{|\t\t|} and
@hc{|\x9;\x9;|} are the same. Note that @code{||} is a valid identifier
that is different from any other identifier.

Here are some examples of identifiers:

@lisp
...                      +
+soup+                   <=?
.>string                 a34kTMNs
lambda                   list->vector
q                        V17a
|two words|              |two\x20;words|
the-word-recursion-has-many-meanings
@end lisp

See section 7.1.1 for the formal syntax of identifiers.

Identifiers have two uses within Scheme programs:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Any identifier can be used as a variable or as a syntactic keyword (see
sections 3.1 and 4.3).

@item
When an identifier appears as a literal or within a literal (see section
4.1.2), it is being used to denote a @emph{symbol} (see section 6.5).
@end itemize

In contrast with earlier revisions of the
report@footnote{@value{refR5RS}}, the syntax distinguishes between upper
and lower case in identifiers and in characters specified using their
names.  However, it does not distinguish between upper and lower case in
numbers, nor in @angle{inline hex escapes} used in the syntax of
iden@value{shy}tifiers, characters, or strings.  None of the identifiers
defined in this report contain upper-case characters, even when they
appear to do so as a result of the English-language convention of
capitalizing the first word of a sentence.

The following directives give explicit control over case folding.

@example
#!fold-case
#!no-fold-case
@end example

These directives can appear anywhere comments are permitted (see section
2.2) but must be followed by a delimiter.  They are treated as comments,
except that they affect the reading of subsequent data from the same
port.  The @code{#!fold-case} directive causes subsequent identifiers
and character names to be case-folded as if by @code{string-foldcase}
(see section 6.7). It has no effect on character literals.  The
@code{#!no-fold-case} directive causes a re@value{shy}turn to the
default, non-folding behavior.

@node Whitespace and comments
@section Whitespace and comments

@define{Whitespace} characters include the space, tab, and newline
characters.  (Im@value{shy}plemen@value{shy}tations may provide
additional whitespace characters such as page break.)  Whitespace is
used for improved readability and as necessary to separate tokens from
each other, a token being an indivisible lexical unit such as an
identifier or number, but is otherwise insignificant.  Whitespace can
occur between any two tokens, but not within a token.  Whitespace
occurring inside a string or inside a symbol delimited by vertical lines
is significant.

The lexical syntax includes several comment forms.  Comments are treated
exactly like whitespace.

A semicolon (@code{;}) indicates the start of a line comment.  The
comment con@value{shy}tinues to the end of the line on which the
semicolon appears.  Another way to indicate a comment is to prefix a
@angle{datum} (cf. section 7.1.2) with @code{@hashchar{};} and optional
@angle{whitespace}.  The comment consists of the comment prefix
@code{@hashchar{};}, the space, and the @angle{datum} together.  This
notation is useful for @quotedbl{commenting out} sections of code.

Block comments are indicated with properly nested
@code{@hashchar{}@U{007C}} and @code{@U{007C}@hashchar{}} pairs.

@lisp
#|
   The FACT procedure computes the factorial
   of a non-negative integer.
|#
(define fact
  (lambda (n)
    (if (= n 0)
        #;(= n 1)
        1        ;Base case: return 1
        (* n (fact (- n 1))))))
@end lisp

@node Other notations
@section Other notations

For a description of the notations used for numbers, see section 6.2.

@table @t
@item . + -
These are used in numbers, and can also occur anywhere in an identifier.
A delimited plus or minus sign by itself is also an identifier.  A
delimited period (not occurring within a number or identifier) is used
in the notation for pairs (section 6.4), and to indicate a
rest-parameter in a formal parameter list (section 4.1.4).  Note that a
sequence of two or more periods @emph{is} an identifier.

@item ( )
Parentheses are used for grouping and to notate lists (section 6.4).

@item '
The apostrophe (single quote) character is used to indicate literal data
(section 4.1.2).

@item `
The grave accent (backquote) character is used to indicate partly
constant data (section 4.2.8).

@item @comma{} @comma{}@atchar{}
The character comma and the sequence comma at-sign are used in
conjunction with quasiquotation (section 4.2.8).

@item "
The quotation mark character is used to delimit strings (section 6.7).

@item @backslashchar{}
Backslash is used in the syntax for character constants (section 6.6)
and as an escape character within string constants (section 6.7) and
identifiers (section 7.1.1).

@item [ ] @lbracechar{} @rbracechar{}
Left and right square and curly brackets (braces) are reserved for
possible future extensions to the language.

@item @hashchar{}
The number sign is used for a variety of purposes depending on the
character that immediately follows it:

@item @hashchar{}t @hashchar{}f
These are the boolean constants (section 6.3), along with the
alternatives @code{@hashchar{}true} @code{@hashchar{}false}.

@item @hashchar{}@backslashchar{}
This introduces a character constant (section 6.6).

@item @hashchar{}(
This introduces a vector constant (section 6.8).  Vector constants are
terminated by @code{)} .

@item @hashchar{}u8(
This introduces a bytevector constant (section 6.8).  Bytevector
constants are terminated by @code{)} .

@item @hashchar{}e @hashchar{}i @hashchar{}b @hashchar{}o @hashchar{}d @hashchar{}x
These are used in the notation for numbers (section 6.2.5).

@item @hashchar{}@sv{n}= @hashchar{}@sv{n}@hashchar{}
These are used for labeling and referencing other literal data (section
2.4).
@end table

@node Datum labels
@section Datum labels

@defblock
@deftypeline {lexical syntax} @/ @prosedatumlabel{n}@sv{datum}
@deftypeline {lexical syntax} @/ @prosedatumref{n}

The lexical syntax @prosedatumlabel{n}@angle{datum} reads the same as
@angle{datum}, but also results in @angle{datum} being labeled by
@angle{n}.  It is an error if @angle{n} is not a sequence of digits.

The lexical syntax @prosedatumref{n} serves as a reference to some
object labeled by @prosedatumlabel{n}; the result is the same object as
the @prosedatumlabel{n} (see section 6.1).  Together, these syntaxes
permit the notation of structures with shared or circular
sub@value{shy}structure.

@lisp
(let ((x (list 'a 'b 'c)))
  (set-cdr! (cddr x) x)
  x)                       @ok{#0=(a b c . #0#)}
@end lisp

The scope of a datum label is the portion of the outermost datum in
which it appears that is to the right of the label.  Consequently, a
reference @prosedatumref{n} can occur only after a label
@prosedatumlabel{n}; it is an error to attempt a forward reference.  In
addition, it is an error if the reference appears as the labeled object
itself (as in @prosedatumlabel{n}@prosedatumref{n}, because the object
labeled by @prosedatumlabel{n} is not well defined in this case.

It is an error for a @angle{program} or @angle{library} to include
circular references ex@value{shy}cept in literals.  In particular, it is
an error for @code{quasiquote} (section 4.2.8) to contain them.

@lisp
#1=(begin (display #\x) #1#) @lispnote{error}
@end lisp
@end defblock
